<section xml:id="sec-solutions-hw3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Assignment 3</title>

    <p>
    Section: <xref ref="sec-vector-fields" text="title"/>.
    </p>

  <p>
    Work each problem yourself first; the full solution follows immediately
    below it. The auto-graded version of the same problems is
    <xref ref="sec-exercises-hw3-online" text="title"/>.
  </p>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw3-inverse-square">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Sketch the vector field
        <m>\displaystyle\mathbf{F}(x,y) = -\frac{\mathbf{r}}{\lVert \mathbf{r} \rVert^3}</m>
        in the plane, where
        <m>\mathbf{r} = \left\langle x, y \right\rangle</m>. Which of the following statements are true? Select all that apply.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>All the vectors point toward the origin.</li>
          <li>The vectors decrease in length as you move away from the origin.</li>
          <li>All the vectors point away from the origin.</li>
          <li>The vectors increase in length as you move away from the origin.</li>
          <li>The length of each vector is <m>1</m>.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw3-solutions">
      <p>
        Since <m>\mathbf{F} = -\frac{1}{\lVert \mathbf{r} \rVert^3}\,\mathbf{r}</m>
        is a negative multiple of the position vector <m>\mathbf{r}</m>,
        the arrow at each point <m>(x,y) \ne (0,0)</m> points from that
        point toward the origin. Its length is
      </p>
      <me>
        \lVert \mathbf{F} \rVert
        = \frac{\lVert \mathbf{r} \rVert}{\lVert \mathbf{r} \rVert^3}
        = \frac{1}{\lVert \mathbf{r} \rVert^2} ,
      </me>
      <p>
        which decreases as you move away from the origin (and is not
        <m>1</m>). So the true statements are: all the vectors point toward
        the origin, and they decrease in length as you move away from it.
        This is an inverse-square field, like gravity; it is not defined at
        the origin.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw3-segment-arc">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Curves <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> are parametrized as follows:
        <m>C_1</m> is <m>(x(t),y(t)) = (t,0)</m> for <m>-1 \le t \le 1</m>,
        and <m>C_2</m> is <m>(x(t),y(t)) = (\cos t, \sin t)</m> for
        <m>0 \le t \le \pi</m>. Sketch, on a separate sheet of paper,
        the curves <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> with arrows showing their
        orientation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Next, suppose that
        <m>\mathbf{F} = 3x\,\mathbf{i} + (4x + 2y)\,\mathbf{j}</m>.
        Calculate <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>,
        where <m>C</m> is the curve <m>C = C_1 \cup C_2</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw3-solutions">
      <p>
        The figure below shows the curves: <m>C_1</m> runs along the
        <m>x</m>-axis from <m>(-1,0)</m> to <m>(1,0)</m>, and <m>C_2</m>
        runs along the upper half of the unit circle from <m>(1,0)</m>
        back to <m>(-1,0)</m>.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>The curves <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> with their orientation.</caption>
        <image width="55%">
          <shortdescription>
            A segment along the x axis from minus one to one, then the upper
            half of the unit circle back to minus one.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              The segment C one runs along the x axis from the point minus
              one comma zero to the point one comma zero, with an arrow
              pointing right. The curve C two is the upper half of the unit
              circle from one comma zero back to minus one comma zero, with
              an arrow at the top pointing left.
            </p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2.4, line join=round, line cap=round]
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oiorange}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  % axes
  \draw[-{Stealth[length=7pt]}, black!70, line width=0.7pt]
    (-1.5,0) -- (1.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
  \draw[-{Stealth[length=7pt]}, black!70, line width=0.7pt]
    (0,-0.35) -- (0,1.45) node[above] {$y$};
  % C1: the segment from (-1,0) to (1,0)
  \draw[oiblue, line width=2pt] (-1,0) -- (1,0);
  \draw[oiblue, line width=2pt, -{Stealth[length=10pt, width=8pt]}]
    (-0.62,0) -- (-0.38,0);
  \node[oiblue, below] at (-0.5,-0.04) {$C_1$};
  % C2: the upper half of the unit circle, from (1,0) to (-1,0)
  \draw[oiorange, line width=2pt] (1,0) arc[start angle=0, end angle=180, radius=1];
  \draw[oiorange, line width=2pt, -{Stealth[length=10pt, width=8pt]}]
    ({cos(80)},{sin(80)}) arc[start angle=80, end angle=100, radius=1];
  \node[oiorange, above right] at ({cos(45)},{sin(45)}) {$C_2$};
  % end points
  \fill (-1,0) circle (0.03) node[below left] {$(-1,0)$};
  \fill (1,0) circle (0.03) node[below right] {$(1,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <p>
        On <m>C_1</m> we have <m>y = 0</m> and <m>d\mathbf{r} = \mathbf{i}\,dx</m>,
        so only the <m>\mathbf{i}</m>-component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m>
        contributes:
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_{C_1} \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \int_{-1}^{1} 3x\,dx = \frac{3x^2}{2}\Big|_{-1}^{1} = 0 .
      </me>
      <p>
        On <m>C_2</m>,
        <m>\mathbf{r}'(t) = -\sin t\,\mathbf{i} + \cos t\,\mathbf{j}</m>, so
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \amp \int_{C_2} \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \int_0^{\pi} \big(3\cos t\,\mathbf{i}
            + (4\cos t + 2\sin t)\,\mathbf{j}\big)
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \quad \cdot \left(-\sin t\,\mathbf{i} + \cos t\,\mathbf{j}\right) dt
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \int_0^{\pi} \left(-\cos t\sin t + 4\cos^2 t\right) dt
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = 2\pi .
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        Hence
        <m>\int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r} = 0 + 2\pi = 2\pi \approx 6.283</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw3-constant-field">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Sketch the vector field
        <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 3\,\mathbf{i} + 4\,\mathbf{j}</m> and
        calculate the line integral of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> along the line
        segment from <m>(-1,3)</m> to <m>(4,1)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw3-solutions">
      <p>
        The field is constant: every arrow is the same vector
        <m>\left\langle 3, 4 \right\rangle</m>, of length <m>5</m>,
        pointing up and to the right. Parametrize the segment by
        <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle -1 + 5t,\ 3 - 2t \right\rangle</m>,
        <m>0 \le t \le 1</m>, so
        <m>\mathbf{r}'(t) = \left\langle 5, -2 \right\rangle</m> and
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
          \amp = \int_0^1 (3 \cdot 5 + 4 \cdot (-2))\,dt
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = 7 .
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        For a constant field this is just <m>\mathbf{F}</m> dotted with
        the displacement <m>\left\langle 5, -2 \right\rangle</m> from the
        start of the segment to its end.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw3-helix-twice">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> for
        <m>\mathbf{F} = 3y\,\mathbf{i} - 2x\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
        if <m>C</m> is the helix <m>x = \cos t</m>, <m>y = \sin t</m>,
        <m>z = t</m>, for <m>0 \le t \le 4\pi</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw3-solutions">
      <p>
        Since <m>C</m> is given by
        <m>\mathbf{r} = \cos t\,\mathbf{i} + \sin t\,\mathbf{j} + t\,\mathbf{k}</m>,
        we have
        <m>\mathbf{r}'(t) = -\sin t\,\mathbf{i} + \cos t\,\mathbf{j} + \mathbf{k}</m>.
        Thus
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \amp \int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \quad = \int_0^{4\pi} \left(3\sin t\,\mathbf{i}
            - 2\cos t\,\mathbf{j} + t\,\mathbf{k}\right)
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \qquad\qquad \cdot \left(-\sin t\,\mathbf{i}
            + \cos t\,\mathbf{j} + \mathbf{k}\right) dt
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \quad = \int_0^{4\pi} \left(-3\sin^2 t - 2\cos^2 t + t\right) dt
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \quad = -6\pi - 4\pi + 8\pi^2 = 8\pi^2 - 10\pi
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \quad \approx 47.541 .
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw3-sign">
    <statement>
      <p>
      Determine whether the line integral of the vector field
      <m>\mathbf{F}</m> along the curve <m>C</m> is positive, negative,
      or zero. <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and <m>C</m> are shown in
      <xref ref="fig-hw3-field"/>. Remember to fully justify your answer.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw3-solutions">
      <p>
      Along the bottom of <m>C</m> the field and the unit tangent make
      acute angles, so <m>\mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{T}} \gt 0</m>
      there; along the top they make obtuse angles, so
      <m>\mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{T}} \lt 0</m>. The field is
      longer<mdash/>stronger<mdash/>over the top half, so the negative
      contribution wins and the integral is <term>negative</term>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <figure xml:id="fig-hw3-field">
      <caption>
        The vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and the curve <m>C</m> of
        <xref ref="ex-hw3-sign"/>. The curve is traversed in the direction of
        the arrowhead.
      </caption>
      <image width="70%">
        <shortdescription>
        A field over the first quadrant tilting from downward to rightward, with a blue circle.
        </shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-hw3-field">
          <diagram dimensions="(380,320)" margins="10">
            <definition>F(x,y) = (0.30*y, -0.30)</definition>
            <definition>circle(t) = (1.5 + 1.05*cos(t), 1.35 + 1.05*sin(t))</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-0.35,-0.35,3.35,2.95)">
              <axes at="hf-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
              <repeat at="hf-cols" parameter="i=0..6">
                <repeat parameter="j=0..5">
                  <vector at="hf-v-((i))-((j))" v="F(0.45*i+0.15, 0.5*j+0.1)" tail="(0.45*i+0.15, 0.5*j+0.1)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
                </repeat>
              </repeat>
              <parametric-curve at="hf-C" function="circle" domain="(0,2*pi)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
              <vector at="hf-Cdir" v="(0.02,0.30)" tail="(2.54,1.15)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
              <label at="hf-Clabel" anchor="(1.5,1.35)" alignment="center" color="#0072B2"><m>C</m></label>
              <label at="hf-Flabel" anchor="(2.75,2.70)" alignment="e" color="#009E73"><m>\mathbf{F}</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure" text="A grid of green field vectors over the first quadrant, together with a blue circle. Near the x axis the field vectors point almost straight down and are short. Higher up they tilt to the right and grow longer, so the field is strongest across the top of the circle. The circle is traversed counterclockwise, and its right side carries an arrowhead pointing upward.">
                <annotation ref="hf-C" text="The closed curve C, a circle, traversed in the direction of the arrowhead on its right side, counterclockwise."/>
                <annotation ref="hf-Cdir" text="The arrowhead giving the direction of travel along C, pointing upward on the right side of the circle."/>
                <annotation ref="hf-cols" text="The field vectors. They are short and nearly vertical near the x axis and grow longer and more tilted as y increases."/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw3-two-paths">
    <statement>
      <p>
      Two particles move from <m>(-1,1)</m> to <m>(1,1)</m> under the
      influence of a force field
      <m>\mathbf{F} = \left\langle y, x \right\rangle</m>. One moves
      along the curve <m>C_1</m>, the parabola defined by
      <m>y = 2x^2 - 1</m>. The other particle moves along the curve
      <m>C_2</m>, the bottom half of the circle
      <m>x^2 + (y-1)^2 = 1</m>. Force is measured in pounds and distances
      are measured in feet. Find the work performed by moving each
      particle along its path.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw3-solutions">
      <p>
      On <m>C_1</m>, <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle t, 2t^2-1
      \right\rangle</m> with <m>-1 \le t \le 1</m>, so
      <m>\mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{v} = 6t^2 - 1</m> and
      <m>W = \int_{-1}^{1}(6t^2-1)\,dt = 2</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
      On <m>C_2</m>, <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle \cos t,
      1+\sin t \right\rangle</m> with <m>\pi \le t \le 2\pi</m>,
      so <m>\mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{v} = \cos(2t) - \sin t</m> and
      <m>W = 2</m> again.
      </p>

      <p>
      The two answers agree because
      <m>\mathbf{F} = \nabla(xy)</m> is conservative<mdash/>revisit this
      problem after <xref ref="section-conservative-fields" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw3-three-forms">
    <statement>
      <p>
      The work done by a force field
      <m>\mathbf{F} = \left\langle M(x,y,z), N(x,y,z), P(x,y,z)
      \right\rangle</m> along a curve <m>C</m> is given by
      <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{T}}\,ds</m>,
      where <m>\hat{\mathbf{T}}</m> is the unit tangent vector to
      <m>C</m>. Show that
      <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{T}}\,ds</m>
      can be written in each of the following forms. Remember to fully
      justify your answers.
      </p>
      <p>
      (a) <m>\displaystyle\int_{t=a}^{t=b} \mathbf{F} \cdot
      \mathbf{v}\,dt</m>
      </p>
      <p>
      (b) <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>
      </p>
      <p>
      (c) <m>\displaystyle\int_C M\,dx + N\,dy + P\,dz</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw3-solutions">
      <p>
        Parametrize <m>C</m> by <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m>, <m>a \le t \le b</m>,
        with velocity <m>\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{r}'(t)</m>. Arc length along
        <m>C</m> satisfies <m>ds = \lVert\mathbf{v}\rVert\,dt</m>, and the
        unit tangent is
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{T}} = \dfrac{\mathbf{v}}{\lVert\mathbf{v}\rVert}</m>
        wherever <m>\mathbf{v} \ne \mathbf{0}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (a) Substituting both of these into the definition, the lengths
        cancel:
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{T}}\,ds
        = \int_{t=a}^{t=b} \mathbf{F}\bigl(\mathbf{r}(t)\bigr) \cdot
          \frac{\mathbf{v}}{\lVert\mathbf{v}\rVert}\,
          \lVert\mathbf{v}\rVert\,dt
        = \int_{t=a}^{t=b} \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{v}\,dt .
      </me>
      <p>
        (b) Since <m>\mathbf{v} = \dfrac{d\mathbf{r}}{dt}</m>, we have
        <m>\mathbf{v}\,dt = d\mathbf{r}</m>, so the integral in (a) is
        exactly
        <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>. This form
        makes no reference to the parameter, which is why it is the one
        usually quoted: the value depends on <m>C</m> and its direction,
        not on how <m>C</m> is parametrized.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) Writing the two vectors in components,
        <m>\mathbf{F} = \langle M, N, P\rangle</m> and
        <m>d\mathbf{r} = \langle dx, dy, dz\rangle</m>, the dot product is
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r} = M\,dx + N\,dy + P\,dz ,
      </me>
      <p>
        so <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \int_C M\,dx + N\,dy + P\,dz</m>, the differential form. All
        three expressions are the same number, written three ways.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw3-helix">
    <statement>
      <p>
      Suppose that <m>C</m> is the portion of the helix given by
      <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle \cos(t), \sin(t), t/2\pi
      \right\rangle</m>, where <m>0 \le t \le 2\pi</m>. Calculate
      <m>\displaystyle\int_C x\,dy - y\,dx + dz</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw3-solutions">
      <p>
      The integral is <m>\int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> with
      <m>\mathbf{F} = \left\langle -y, x, 1 \right\rangle</m>. Along
      the helix <m>\mathbf{F} = \left\langle -\sin t, \cos t, 1
      \right\rangle</m> and
      <m>\mathbf{v} = \left\langle -\sin t, \cos t, \frac{1}{2\pi}
      \right\rangle</m>, so
      <m>\mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{v} = 1 + \frac{1}{2\pi}</m> and
      </p>

      <me>
      \int_C x\,dy - y\,dx + dz
      = \int_0^{2\pi}\left(1 + \frac{1}{2\pi}\right) dt
      = 2\pi + 1 \approx 7.283 .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw3-less-work">
    <statement>
      <p>
      The force field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is shown by the arrows in
      <xref ref="fig-hw3-paths"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
      (a) Over which of the two paths, <m>ADC</m> or <m>ABC</m>, does
      <m>\mathbf{F}</m> perform less work? Fully justify your answer.
      </p>
      <p>
      (b) Is <m>\mathbf{F}</m> a conservative field or not? Why?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw3-solutions">
      <p>
      Both paths run along two sides of the square. The horizontal sides
      <m>AD</m> and <m>BC</m> contribute the same, but the vertical side
      <m>DC</m> sits where the field is longer than it is along
      <m>AB</m>, and the angle between <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and
      <m>\hat{\mathbf{T}}</m> is the same on both. Hence
      <m>\int_{ADC} \gt \int_{ABC}</m>, so <m>ABC</m> is the path of
      less work.
      </p>

      <p>
      Because the two paths share their endpoints and give different
      work, <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is <term>not</term> conservative: a
      conservative field is path-independent.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <figure xml:id="fig-hw3-paths">
      <caption>
        The vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and the two paths <m>ADC</m> and
        <m>ABC</m> of <xref ref="ex-hw3-less-work"/>. Every field vector
        points in the same direction; only their lengths change.
      </caption>
      <image width="70%">
        <shortdescription>
        A uniform slanted field with a square path from corner A to corner B.
        </shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-hw3-paths">
          <diagram dimensions="(360,320)" margins="10">
            <definition>G(x,y) = (0.16*(1+x), 0.16*(1+x))</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-0.3,-0.3,3.3,3.0)">
              <axes at="hp-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
              <repeat at="hp-cols" parameter="i=0..6">
                <repeat parameter="j=0..5">
                  <vector at="hp-v-((i))-((j))" v="G(0.45*i+0.12, 0.5*j+0.12)" tail="(0.45*i+0.12, 0.5*j+0.12)" stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
                </repeat>
              </repeat>
              <polygon at="hp-square" closed="yes" stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3" fill="none" points="((0.8,0.7),(2.4,0.7),(2.4,2.3),(0.8,2.3))"/>
              <point at="hp-A" p="(0.8,0.7)" fill="black" alignment="sw">
                <m>A</m>
              </point>
              <point at="hp-D" p="(2.4,0.7)" fill="black" alignment="se">
                <m>D</m>
              </point>
              <point at="hp-C" p="(2.4,2.3)" fill="black" alignment="ne">
                <m>C</m>
              </point>
              <point at="hp-B" p="(0.8,2.3)" fill="black" alignment="nw">
                <m>B</m>
              </point>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure" text="A grid of light blue field vectors, all pointing up and to the right at the same angle, with lengths that grow as x increases. A magenta square is drawn with corners A at the lower left, D at the lower right, C at the upper right and B at the upper left, so the path A D C runs along the bottom and up the right side, while A B C runs up the left side and along the top.">
                <annotation ref="hp-cols" text="The field vectors, all parallel and pointing up and to the right, growing longer toward the right of the picture."/>
                <annotation ref="hp-square" text="The square whose corners are A, B, C and D; the two paths of the problem run along its sides."/>
                <annotation ref="hp-A" text="The corner A, at the lower left."/>
                <annotation ref="hp-D" text="The corner D, at the lower right; the path A D C passes through it."/>
                <annotation ref="hp-C" text="The corner C, at the upper right."/>
                <annotation ref="hp-B" text="The corner B, at the upper left; the path A B C passes through it."/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

</section>
